<section id="frame">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="eyebrow display"><span class="chap">08</span> Intake</div>
    <h1 class="display doc-title">GitHub is where reporters are. The hive is where work is.</h1>
    <p class="lede-sm">Nothing crosses between them without an operator. This page is the
    loop a GitHub issue goes through once a hive decides to field it — and the boundary that
    keeps agents from ever speaking for you.</p>
    <p>A hive already has an intake queue: <code>bh report</code> and <code>bh escalate</code>
    file into it, <code>bh work intake</code> reads it, and four typed verbs dispose of it (the
    <a href="/fleet/#hq-intake">Fleet page</a> covers that path). This page is the larger
    inbound channel. GitHub Issues can be <b>pulled</b> into a hive as held, untriaged
    reports; triaged with the same verbs as any other report; <b>linked</b> to the internal
    work that addresses them; and <b>kept informed</b> through a supervised outbox, where
    every comment or label change is a draft an operator must read, approve and send.</p>
    <IntakeLoop />
  </div>
</section>

<section id="optin">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="display">Opt-in, and the approval boundary <span class="status shipped">shipped · opt-in</span></h2>
    <p>Everything on this page beyond the plain queue and its four dispositions is
    <b>off by default</b>. Upgrading changes nothing until an operator turns a switch on, and
    with every default in place <code>bh report</code>, merge and release behave exactly as
    they always have. The switches are host-owned — a fleet-level config push cannot flip them
    — and each stage has its own, so a hive can pull issues in without ever drafting a reply,
    or draft replies without ever projecting labels. They live under two sections of the
    <a href="https://github.com/beadhive/beadhive/blob/main/docs/CONFIGURATION.md">configuration
    reference</a>: <code>intake.external</code> for the loop itself and
    <code>plugins.tracker-github</code> for the adapter that talks to GitHub.</p>
    <p>The boundary is stated once here and holds everywhere below:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Reads are read-only.</b> Import, discovery and thread ingestion never write to
      the issue. Credentials are resolved per operation from the environment or the
      <code>gh</code> CLI and never stored by Beadhive.</li>
      <li><b>There are exactly two possible writes:</b> post a comment, and change labels under
      a prefix Beadhive owns. There is no operation to open, close, edit or transfer an issue.</li>
      <li><b>A write needs three independent things.</b> The adapter's own write flag, the
      outbox switch, and a per-operation, single-use grant minted by an operator approving the
      digest of the exact rendered bytes. No config key, at any layer, can enable or pre-approve
      a write.</li>
      <li><b>Agents cannot approve.</b> Developer, dispatcher, crew and coordinator seats can
      triage, link and draft; none of them can approve, and timers, config, environment
      variables and flags cannot produce an approval either.</li>
      <li><b><code>bh</code> makes no LLM call</b> anywhere in intake or triage. Where analysis is
      needed, it is supplied from outside and handed in as a document.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="note"><b>Nothing is sent without an operator.</b> Every reply, question, status
    note and label change is a draft first. An operator reads the exact text, approves it, and
    sends it. There is no auto-reply, and nothing a reporter sees was composed by a model
    inside Beadhive.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="pull">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="display">Pulling issues in <span class="status shipped">shipped · opt-in</span></h2>
    <p><code>bh work import &lt;issue-url&gt;</code> brings one issue in as an untriaged report
    with <code>origin:github</code>. It is keyed on the issue's canonical URL, so importing it
    again updates the stored snapshot rather than duplicating it, and never resets triage. A
    dry run classifies the URL and writes nothing. Closed issues import exactly like open ones;
    pull requests, issues in other projects and anything not visible to the credential are
    refused before any request is made.</p>
    <p><code>bh work discover</code> is the same upsert run over the repository. The incremental
    pass resumes from a checkpoint that advances only once a page is durably stored, so a
    re-run changes nothing; a full pass re-pulls everything, closed issues included, and marks
    a report whose issue has vanished as missing — it never deletes or closes one. The host
    daemon can run discovery on a timer; that needs its own switch and an explicit interval,
    and the daemon refuses to start if the adapter is not wired.</p>
    <p>An imported report is <b>held</b>. It carries a hold gate for life: it never appears in
    ready work, <code>bh work claim</code> and <code>assign</code> refuse it, and no seat can
    resolve the hold. A report is never the work. The work that addresses it is a separate
    bead the report tracks — which is what keeps a bug report from being handed to a developer
    as if it were a specification. <code>bh doctor</code> sweeps for missing or orphaned holds
    in any hive whose store binds a tracker adapter.</p>
    <p>Two things that look similar and are not: discovery here means polling GitHub for
    issues, not finding candidate work for the planner; and pulling issues into intake is
    distinct from the public-store projection (<code>bh hive remote sync … --pull-only</code>),
    which mirrors a hive's GitHub Issues into its public bead store. Intake reads GitHub itself
    through the tracker port and never reads or links that projection.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="triage">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="display">Triage, and waiting on the reporter <span class="status shipped">shipped</span></h2>
    <p>A GitHub-bound report sits in the same queue as a cross-hive report or an escalation,
    and the same four verbs dispose of it: <code>bh work accept</code> clears it into the
    backlog, <code>bh work reject</code> closes it with a recorded reason, <code>bh work
    reroute</code> re-files it into the right hive or bounces it to a superintendent, and
    <code>bh work promote</code> hands it to the planner, whose <code>bh plan adopt</code>
    seeds a molecule from it. Likely duplicates are flagged when the queue is read; batch triage
    (below) clusters them. One queue for every channel means one triage and one set of verbs.</p>
    <p>Two things change for a report that came from GitHub. First, a plain
    <code>reject</code> is a local close — the reason is recorded on the bead, and telling the
    reporter is a separate drafted, operator-approved comment. Second, the report can
    <b>wait</b>. <code>bh work needs-info &lt;report&gt; --ask "…"</code> drafts the question as a
    supervised reply and moves the report out of the untriaged queue (still held, never
    claimable); <code>bh work wake</code> moves it back when the ingested thread holds a reply
    from a <em>human</em> — our own posted messages never wake it, and the reply stays unread for
    the next triage pass. Every disposition fields a waiting report exactly as it fields an
    untriaged one. Both verbs are opt-in.</p>
    <p>The thread itself is read with three verbs, all read-only toward GitHub and all opt-in.
    <code>bh work thread ingest</code> pulls a bound report's comments since a checkpoint into a
    private record and recognises Beadhive's own posted messages so they are never mistaken
    for new evidence. <code>bh work thread show</code> renders what is stored — which comments
    are new since triage last read, and both kinds of link — without a request.
    <code>bh work thread considered --through &lt;seq&gt;</code> records that triage has read the
    thread that far; it never changes the report's state. A deleted comment is only tombstoned
    after repeated snapshots agree it is gone.</p>
    <IntakeRouting />
  </div>
</section>

<section id="track">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="display">Tracking versus investigating <span class="status shipped">shipped · opt-in</span></h2>
    <p>Two kinds of link, kept deliberately apart. <code>bh work link &lt;report&gt; --to
    &lt;work&gt;</code> makes the report <b>track</b> a work bead: several reports can track the
    same fix, the report stays held, and nothing is sent. <code>bh work accept --work &lt;id&gt;</code>
    or <code>--new-work</code> does the accept and the link in one step; the fresh work bead has
    no GitHub binding and a description written by triage, not copied from the issue. A link is
    refused if the target is itself a bound report, lives in another store, is still untriaged,
    or is already tracked by something else (unless replaced on purpose).</p>
    <p><code>bh work investigate</code> attaches <b>reproduction</b> work with a different edge.
    Claiming, closing or merging an investigating bead drafts no milestone, writes no delivery
    state and never closes the report — so a hive can spend effort finding out whether a report
    is real without telling the reporter anything until there is something true to say.
    Several beads may investigate one report; only one piece of work can be the one it tracks.</p>
    <p>What finishes a report is a hive choice: by default it closes once the work it tracks
    has merged; a hive can instead keep it open and held until the fix is verified to be in a
    release.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="outbox">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="display">The outbox <span class="status shipped">shipped · opt-in</span></h2>
    <p>The outbox is the <em>only</em> way anything leaves toward GitHub. <code>bh work outbox
    draft &lt;report&gt; --text "…"</code> drafts a reply and sends nothing. <code>bh work outbox
    show &lt;id&gt;</code> renders the exact message and prints its digest. <code>bh work outbox
    approve &lt;id&gt; --op &lt;kind&gt;:&lt;digest&gt;</code> is the operator's act: it approves that
    digest and mints one single-use grant per operation — a comment approval never approves a
    label change — and still sends nothing. <code>bh work outbox send &lt;id&gt;</code> posts one
    approved operation, write-ahead with a receipt, and only if the fresh render still matches
    the approved digest; if the text has drifted since approval, nothing is posted.</p>
    <p>A <b>leak check</b> runs at draft, at every render and on the exact bytes at send. Secrets,
    a label outside the owned prefix, and the absence of a bound sanitizer block outright and
    cannot be overridden. Internal bead ids, branch names, private labels, seat names and host
    paths block unless an operator names that specific finding as deliberately published. A
    closing keyword like <code>fixes #N</code> draws a warning. The failure modes are handled
    without re-sending: <code>reconcile</code> settles an unknown outcome by reading the issue
    back, <code>discard</code> abandons an unsent operation with a reason, and
    <code>retry</code> returns a failed one to drafted, where it needs a new render, digest and
    approval.</p>

    <h3 class="display" id="milestones">Delivery milestones <span class="status shipped">opt-in · each its own switch</span></h3>
    <p>Milestones are how the reporter learns what happened without anyone composing a message.
    Each one <em>only drafts</em> a record in the outbox: it never sends and never mints a grant.
    The wording is a fixed constant per event, so a milestone carries no bead id, branch, path
    or seat by construction, and the hook that writes them is a no-op unless intake is on and
    can never fail a merge, release or claim.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Milestone</th><th>Drafted when</th><th>What the reporter would read</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>started</code></td><td>the first claim of work the report tracks (a re-claim or resume drafts nothing)</td><td>work to address this issue has started</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>blocked</code></td><td>an operator runs <code>bh work delivery blocked &lt;work&gt; --reason</code> with a public reason that passes the leak check</td><td>a fixed lead plus the operator's reason</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>deprioritised</code></td><td>an operator runs <code>bh work delivery deprioritised &lt;work&gt; --reason</code></td><td>a fixed lead plus the operator's reason</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>merged</code></td><td>the tracked work merges; every tracking report moves to <code>delivery:merged</code></td><td>a change addressing this issue has merged and will be in an upcoming release</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>released</code></td><td>an operator runs <code>bh work delivery release &lt;tag&gt;</code> and read-only git verifies the tag contains the merge; "no" and "unknown" change nothing</td><td>a change addressing this issue is available in that release</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Progress notices draft only while delivery is still pending, a newer milestone supersedes
    an unsent older one, and a report linked late to work that has already merged can catch up
    on what it missed — also a switch, off by default.</p>

    <h3 class="display" id="labels">The label projection <span class="status shipped">opt-in</span></h3>
    <p>A hive can mirror its own state onto GitHub labels under one prefix it owns
    (<code>bh:</code> by default) — intake state, delivery state, type, priority and the rest,
    each as <code>bh:&lt;dimension&gt;/&lt;value&gt;</code>. Labels outside that prefix are never
    added, removed or even reported on, and every projected name and description goes through
    the leak check. The projection switch by itself reads and writes nothing; it only shapes
    the catalog.</p>
    <p>The verbs follow the outbox's shape. <code>bh work labels define</code> renders a plan for
    the repository's label <em>definitions</em> with a digest, and an operator applies it from
    an operator seat with one grant per write, audited. <code>bh work labels sync</code> drafts
    at most one label-change record per issue, converging its owned labels onto bead state — it
    refuses to add a label the repository has not defined. <code>bh work labels review</code>
    renders the drafted batch; <code>bh work labels approve --batch</code> is the operator's act,
    minting one grant per issue and skipping any issue whose live labels moved since the draft;
    and a send is still <code>bh work outbox send … --kind labels</code>. Humans editing those
    labels by hand win until the next milestone. Optionally, every disposition, link and
    milestone refreshes the report's draft too — drafts only, never failing the transition.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="batch">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="display">Review-gated batch triage <span class="status shipped">shipped · opt-in</span></h2>
    <p>For a backlog rather than a trickle. <code>bh work triage review --pending</code> builds
    review packets: each report's body <em>and its full thread</em>, any stored proposal with
    its reviews, its duplicate cluster, and a global index of titles and dispositions. Clusters
    are never split across batches, and a packet without its full thread is refused, so the
    reviewer never judges a report with half the evidence. Duplicate families are found three
    independent ways: the store's own duplicate scan, text similarity, and a code path both
    reports' evidence cites.</p>
    <p>An analyst — an agent or a person, <em>outside</em> <code>bh</code> — reads a packet and
    writes a proposal; <code>bh work triage propose &lt;report&gt; &lt;file&gt;</code> stores it as a
    private document on the report, leak-linted first and never published. A verifier hands in
    a verdict the same way: confirmed, corrected or unsure. <code>bh</code> stores and checks;
    it never calls a model.</p>
    <p><code>bh work triage check</code> runs deterministic, fail-closed checks against the
    repository before anything is applied: the targets a proposal names exist and are open, a
    claimed fix's commits are ancestors of the integration branch and the fix version is
    <em>computed</em> from tags rather than taken on trust, duplicate families resolve, reporter-
    facing text is leak-clean and within length, and nothing has gone stale underneath the
    analysis. Then <code>bh work triage apply --reviewed</code> — operator-run, with a dry run
    that writes nothing — applies only proposals a reviewer confirmed and which pass every
    check. Each maps onto the ordinary triage verbs, so every refusal above still holds; any
    reply or question it produces is a draft in the outbox; a second apply changes nothing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="templates">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="display">Issue forms and the checklist <span class="status shipped">shipped</span></h2>
    <p>Beadhive's own repository ships versioned issue forms for bugs and feature requests,
    each carrying a template marker, with blank issues still allowed and security reports
    pointed at the private advisory flow. Alongside them is an information-sufficiency
    checklist: per type, which facts are required before triage can decide anything (what
    happened, what was expected, how to reproduce, the affected version, the impact) and which
    are optional.</p>
    <p>Two honest limits. The checklist is a document the triage step reads — <code>bh</code> does
    not evaluate it — and the forms are what Beadhive ships for its own tracker, not something
    installed into every hive. The checklist's rule is worth adopting anyway: ask only for the
    facts needed to decide, treat a bare "n/a" as missing, and ask in one message that names the
    investigation the missing fact blocks. The reply is, as always, a draft the operator
    approves.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="direction">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="display">What is still direction <span class="status direction">direction</span></h2>
    <p>Design text that has not become a verb or a switch, listed so the page above cannot be
    read as promising it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Webhook-driven wake-ups.</b> Ingestion today is pull-only polling; nothing is pushed
      into a hive in real time.</li>
      <li><b>Reminders and inactivity closure</b> for a reporter who never answers, and a path for
      reopening. The design says this should be explicit hive policy; no key exists.</li>
      <li><b>Revert detection and a "corrected" notice.</b> The vocabulary has room for it; no verb
      writes it.</li>
      <li><b>Unattended publication.</b> Supervised is the only outbox mode and the only mode the
      config accepts.</li>
      <li><b>Other trackers.</b> The port is tracker-neutral by design; GitHub is the only adapter
      that exists.</li>
      <li><b>Automatic routing by report channel.</b> The descriptor spec ships; nothing reads it yet
      (the dashed branch in the routing diagram above).</li>
      <li><b>An ideation plane.</b> Intake and planner adoption are the shipped parts; deriving
      candidate work from a repo's vision and accumulated evidence has no contract yet — see
      the <a href="/planes/#ideation">Planes page</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

